01:10:00.116 --> 01:10:05.116
And we were still carrying over $800 million
in losses in that year just

01:10:05.116 --> 01:10:09.436
because we were spending a lot of money
scaling up our food delivery business.

01:10:10.076 --> 01:10:15.756
What was challenging was this required us to forecast
not just when we would get out of Covid,

01:10:16.116 --> 01:10:23.796
but also the pace of recovery in terms of both the domestic
mobility business as well as travel inflows back into Southeast Asia.

01:10:24.916 --> 01:10:31.956
Here's a here's a context. Ho Chi Minh City Back
in 2021, Covid had absolutely devastated the region.

01:10:32.356 --> 01:10:39.916
Our ride hailing business, the cash cow for Grab,
had seen bookings collapse nearly 80% in some of our markets.

01:10:40.516 --> 01:10:46.636
Food delivery was surging, but scaling it at the pace
we were doing required considerable funding.

01:10:47.636 --> 01:10:53.636
Governments across eight countries were reopening
at different speeds with different rules,

01:10:53.636 --> 01:10:56.476
and no one could tell us when it would all end.

01:10:56.476 --> 01:11:02.876
Worst of all, our finance data infrastructure at that time was trying
to keep up with the pace of new product developments,

01:11:02.876 --> 01:11:05.396
which led to this explosive deliveries growth.

01:11:06.876 --> 01:11:09.516
What did our scenario planning look like back then?

01:11:09.516 --> 01:11:15.796
I sat in a room with my team and just getting
accurate data alone required verification as well

01:11:15.796 --> 01:11:18.876
as manual reconciliation of our finances.

01:11:19.196 --> 01:11:26.196
We spent weeks doing research, interviewing experts,
speaking to government advisories, studying epidemiological data,

01:11:26.476 --> 01:11:29.836
and every time I went back to leadership, they came back and said,

01:11:29.836 --> 01:11:34.716
now show me what happens if the lockdown lasts
for another six months, not just three.

01:11:34.716 --> 01:11:36.596
And we were back at square one.

01:11:39.516 --> 01:11:43.876
We were making existential decisions for the company,
serving millions of people.

01:11:44.356 --> 01:11:47.796
And our best tool was a spreadsheet with 47 tabs.

01:11:48.756 --> 01:11:55.076
The infrastructure behind those numbers was not where it needed to be,
and that experience left a mark on me.

01:11:55.476 --> 01:12:01.756
Not just the stress of it, but the conviction that we had
to fundamentally change how Grab finance function operated.

01:12:03.476 --> 01:12:11.156
This is when we made a pivotal decision to rebuild our entire
data foundation on AWS as Grab's preferred cloud provider.

01:12:12.036 --> 01:12:19.116
Grab today runs dozens of enterprise systems,
including different SaaS platforms and our own analytics warehouse.

01:12:19.636 --> 01:12:26.836
For years, these systems did not talk to each other,
making it difficult to access and use for machine learning and AI.

01:12:27.356 --> 01:12:29.236
Finance pulls numbers from one source.

01:12:29.236 --> 01:12:32.636
Operations from another, and we spend days reconciling the gap.

01:12:33.516 --> 01:12:37.316
We fixed this with an in-house program called Grab House.

01:12:37.316 --> 01:12:44.156
Now scaling into what we call Project Nucleus,
using Amazon S3 as our storage Foundation.

01:12:44.196 --> 01:12:48.756
Apache iceberg for Open Table Formats
and a medallion data architecture.

01:12:48.756 --> 01:12:55.516
We built a governance centralized data lake that serves
as a single source of truth for the entire company.

01:12:57.396 --> 01:13:00.236
The modernization impact has been tangible.

01:13:00.236 --> 01:13:07.236
By modernizing on AWS, Grab has established a single
reliable source of truth across the organization,

01:13:07.236 --> 01:13:14.716
eliminating data inconsistencies, reducing reconciliation overhead,
and giving every team confidence to act on their data.

01:13:15.276 --> 01:13:23.116
Take support costs, for example. Before this, when we had to figure
out how to allocate the cost of running our support operations,

01:13:23.116 --> 01:13:28.916
we used to allocate this to each of our business
as a rough proxy with Grab house.

01:13:29.316 --> 01:13:33.716
We can now allocate to the level of the individual agent and ticket.

01:13:34.156 --> 01:13:38.236
And when we did that, we enhanced
our allocation process significantly,

01:13:38.236 --> 01:13:43.796
and it allowed our business leaders to make
much better informed decisions as a company.

01:13:44.356 --> 01:13:46.156
And that is not just a rounding error.

01:13:46.436 --> 01:13:53.436
It is how you access unit economics and how you set budgets
and how you allocate capital in what is an industry that is,

01:13:53.476 --> 01:13:55.756
you know, every cent or every penny counts.

01:13:57.956 --> 01:14:01.196
We know you cannot run a profitable company on inaccurate data,

01:14:01.196 --> 01:14:06.996
and building Grab House has allowed us
to drop manual reconciliation by up to 60%.

01:14:07.676 --> 01:14:11.316
Now, reporting cycles that took a month are happening in real time,

01:14:11.516 --> 01:14:16.796
and we have moved 14 of our most
critical finance reports onto the data lake.

01:14:17.196 --> 01:14:19.956
So month end close no longer feels like a fight.

01:14:20.396 --> 01:14:24.796
And and with daily resource daily operations for system resources.

01:14:26.436 --> 01:14:33.396
In 2025, Grab reported its first full year of net profit $200 million,

01:14:33.436 --> 01:14:40.756
while completing over 500 million of monthly
transactions and processing 300TB of data a day,

01:14:41.316 --> 01:14:49.236
and we are well on our path to achieving $1.5
billion of EBITDA by 2028. This is not a turnaround story.

01:14:49.236 --> 01:14:58.716
This is a company that went from near in existence due to the existential
crisis back in 2021 to now growing and accelerating,

01:14:58.716 --> 01:15:00.000
driving, accelerating profitable growth.

